{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790443557,"nonce":"V9pAhvB0jR8hMybLEPa8TFhe7yE=","message":"Landed in 8147196: heap::keep(ptr) returns a Kept guard. On native it stays on the frame, as the black_box did. On wasm it pushes the address onto the thread's kept stack, a Vec in the Tlab that the mutator record points to; the snapshot adds its used part to that mutator's scan ranges. Converted: cell::made (the object through the cell's allocation), caribou-ash callback.rs (the Callback through its closure's allocation), caribou-wren proxy_in (obj) and finish (the result). Buffers whose address is taken (argument arrays passed as slices) live on the shadow stack and are scanned, so they need no guard. Test: cell::tests::a_kept_cell_outlives_a_collection_on_wasm under wasmtime. The control, with the guard keeping nothing, fails 'kept, the cell lives', so the kept stack is what holds it. Core wasm suite 72 passed, 6 ignored; native suite passes. Also found: in a wasm test nothing registers the thread (Ash's AOT main does, through hlp_gc_set_stack_top), so those tests root by handle. Remaining: sites that hold a value in a local across an allocation without saying so. Finding them needs the bridge running on wasm under a collect-on-every-allocation stress mode, which needs 3bfe4a0 (the runtime object) first; caribou-wren does not build for wasm until it uses wren_lift's aot_runtime feature.","files":null}]}